#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166939 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee92df8-a96b-5333-ae8d-b489df88c366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365860} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3b7016-4934-590e-b25b-b91ebf2125b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $\\frac{1}{10}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365861} {:answer/id #uuid "acc5b921-33a3-53bb-931e-2dc15cff77d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{10}$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365862} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8a4734-03dd-59ba-8bf2-3aa5143e433c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f3b7016-4934-590e-b25b-b91ebf2125b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $\\frac{1}{10}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365861} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d0668d-3ca7-569b-925d-2e956888c08a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365859} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f73ef24-52a4-5a30-9cf7-ddacd6a35251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365865} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1b1386-fefe-522a-9724-c7282fdf6830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $C{e}^{-x/2}(x+4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365866} {:answer/id #uuid "e9883a8c-7a90-5bdd-a094-5cb18a2dfc22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x+\\frac{3}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365867} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3aea8a-e9e7-5796-bf9a-ff68da034ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f73ef24-52a4-5a30-9cf7-ddacd6a35251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365865} :answer-field/id #uuid "4984fab2-6b76-5c85-a24b-7e17e049b22f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6e87982-ab6f-5e96-94e7-996ae7f3b605" :question/math-academy-id "q-338845" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-10\\lambda {y}^{′}+25{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-10\\lambda m+25{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-5\\lambda )(m-5\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=5\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{5\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{5\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{5\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5A\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda x}+B{e}^{5\\lambda x}+5B\\lambda x{e}^{5\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5A\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda }+B{e}^{5\\lambda }+5B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 5A\\lambda +B+5B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{10\\lambda }+2B{e}^{10\\lambda }+2(5A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }+B{e}^{10\\lambda }+10B\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(5A\\lambda +B+10B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+10A\\lambda +2B+20B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+(4+20\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+4(1+5\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+4(1+5\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}5\\lambda  & 1+5\\lambda  \\\\ 1+10\\lambda  & 4(1+5\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+5\\lambda )-(1+5\\lambda )(1+10\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )[20\\lambda -(1+10\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )[20\\lambda -1-10\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )(10\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{10}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{10},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166939 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f50d85a-d89e-54a5-9345-420b74f8791d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x-1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312617} {:answer/id #uuid "91c66360-4918-5276-b845-ac44193359a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313141} {:answer/id #uuid "a01393e8-ef2d-593d-a94e-1d1084d26012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313236} {:answer/id #uuid "b1681b33-b5a8-5f0d-92fb-40c5b589cd27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313351} {:answer/id #uuid "bf967032-f5da-588f-a109-6cc6c1aa4eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91c66360-4918-5276-b845-ac44193359a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313141} :answer-field/id #uuid "397d2544-b95c-5cbf-bb7c-86323fbb77f7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314169} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6283b732-8b06-555e-af02-111afd05e681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312760} {:answer/id #uuid "6c638776-b9e4-55f9-9d26-40cc15add57e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{12}$ and $\\frac{1}{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312824} {:answer/id #uuid "e0de4999-e77c-501d-afe0-d29450a96a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{12}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313746} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf7b9e8-ac61-5c15-92e6-336389f2e7b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313844} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d2cc65-9f97-5140-a955-6650f339d413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8d2cc65-9f97-5140-a955-6650f339d413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313963} :answer-field/id #uuid "9748d478-2e0b-5f0e-90d6-30a9ca3bb106" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2709e91-93e6-520b-8d50-587e1e5c8629" :question/math-academy-id "q-338904" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-12\\lambda {y}^{′}+36{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-12\\lambda m+36{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-6\\lambda )(m-6\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=6\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{6\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{6\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda x}+B{e}^{6\\lambda x}+6B\\lambda x{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (6\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+2(6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(6A\\lambda +B+6B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+12A\\lambda +2B+12B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(6\\lambda )A+B=0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+12\\lambda  & 3+12\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda (3+12\\lambda )-(1)(1+12\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 18\\lambda +72{\\lambda }^{2}-1-12\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 72{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (12\\lambda -1)(6\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166940 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22" :knowledge-point/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80717e13-aa04-534e-b7cb-b9dd198b1171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391697} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9a1003-518c-5978-82a3-b12124ff2cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391698} {:answer/id #uuid "cb175292-1d19-53ba-a268-6e1a4ae2b1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391699} {:answer/id #uuid "ab342eb6-9f44-5413-9868-ba9ddfbb18a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391700} {:answer/id #uuid "94b96273-f78c-566b-9d25-97a799b4f55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94b96273-f78c-566b-9d25-97a799b4f55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391701} :answer-field/id #uuid "b89af3df-4153-5000-b2fe-f6e14e30ad1b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391696} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3af5ddc-3890-5c59-8803-f398173deb54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391703} {:answer/id #uuid "0044f4b1-e6d3-557b-b79a-d102edcc257a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391704} {:answer/id #uuid "4961ff2e-c297-5af0-a99e-d7db5b609a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4{\\lambda }_{n}x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391705} {:answer/id #uuid "40f7e995-e510-5677-a3ee-2e9610868cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391706} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c87594-7fd9-5262-b977-690b21e75561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391707} {:answer/id #uuid "56ed424b-8ca9-59d2-8528-1ebfe5b468f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{\\lambda }_{n}x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6c87594-7fd9-5262-b977-690b21e75561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391707} :answer-field/id #uuid "d664b659-e57c-53ea-8b4c-aa060927c521" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b03c659-cc6c-5259-9cfb-c53b896cc809" :question/math-academy-id "q-338909" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter value corresponding to the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x)+4B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+4B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda \\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n4\\lambda \\pi =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166940 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22" :knowledge-point/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d299aab6-7d45-5daf-aeed-dbcaf20eb01f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391722} {:answer/id #uuid "50cf2632-5224-53c3-9780-700b593c95e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391723} {:answer/id #uuid "dad65025-c4f6-50bb-bfc0-81fdab4d27f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391724} {:answer/id #uuid "78ab4f16-e632-5d7c-a0ed-fca61eb6d86b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}\\pi }{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dad65025-c4f6-50bb-bfc0-81fdab4d27f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391724} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3e4d0a8-336d-51eb-9840-10ac5a3bc5ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391721} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "950d017d-4409-5a7d-93e6-82c04aebf61f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391727} {:answer/id #uuid "a76caf84-a8f6-5ed7-b166-b53efe8b63f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391728} {:answer/id #uuid "7e019b9a-dbcd-5aaa-9d4a-e9839bb29f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391729} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae13067-ee42-5ad2-b168-11769b5a9ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e019b9a-dbcd-5aaa-9d4a-e9839bb29f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391729} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf084e4c-8bde-52e0-8415-918ddbdb9317" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55c03286-efa5-5f72-9411-1febab9b4b0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338920" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)=y(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(\\pi )=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n4\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{16}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"}]}
